# Anna in the Budget Debate on 4 July 1957
Tamil title: 4.7.57 அன்று வரவு செலவுத் திட்ட விவாதத்தில் அண்ணா

## Part 1

### Images 1-2

**Anna:** Mr Speaker, the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam, like every other party, has a responsibility to examine the budget that our State Finance Minister has placed before this House for debate. I am genuinely pleased that I have received an opportunity to participate in that examination. Before considering the budget itself, I believe it will be very helpful to examine the circumstances in which it has been presented to us, so that we may properly understand its character. How many circumstances are there altogether in our favour when we make such an examination?

The Finance Minister has happily informed us that nature has helped us this year. Although the people of this country harbour many kinds of grievance, they have remained responsible, law-abiding citizens up to this moment: they pay every tax demanded of them and respect the laws that are enacted. This is indeed a favourable circumstance. Likewise, most of the newspapers in our country support the administration today. Whatever defects there may be in its administration, plans, and policies, and however much these may lack strength or lustre, the newspapers apply attractive coats of paint and make them appear impressive whenever their lustre fades.

That is not all. Today's Ministry is headed by Mr Kamaraj. It has administrative ability, people of acknowledged good faith, and capable ministers. Having dedicated himself to public life, he believes that no party other than his own can accomplish anything; he plans to embrace and destroy some who oppose his party and to eliminate and bind others. Nor is that all: we have inherited an able administrative apparatus from which the entire Indian subcontinent might learn lessons.

And so I did not go searching for the distasteful word "but"; it comes pursuing me of its own accord. But despite all these favourable circumstances, prices have risen. Unemployment is rampant in our country. Shortage pursues us in every direction. Everyone employed in government offices and government-aided offices is dissatisfied today.

No favourable condition is being created to remove that dissatisfaction. The budget states that office employees have received government assistance, but I believe the Minister knows that none of them is content. I remind him of this only to increase the affection and regard he has for them, not merely to find fault. I respectfully submit that the many matters that do call for criticism have already been explained in this House.

### Images 3-4

This House has not been convened merely to let each person display his or her oratorical ability. One person raises one matter and someone answers it: that is what has happened here. I remind both the members and myself that it ought not to remain so. Members of the Opposition state every charge properly and give their reasons. They possess the ability to uphold a policy and explain it with suitable evidence, discipline, and dignity; this is especially true of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam.

I wish to draw the attention of this House to what is happening in the other legislatures of the Indian subcontinent. A few days ago we read in the newspapers that a man defeated in an election was appointed Governor, and that a great uproar opposed the address he delivered in the legislature. Governor Prakasa, who had discharged his office in our State with distinction and ability, went to address the Bombay Legislature, where a major disturbance arose over the demand that there should be no bilingual state.

Above all, Mr Speaker, when the Speaker of the Rajasthan Legislative Assembly was absent, a member named Trilok Singh climbed into the Speaker's chair and refused to move. At a time when all these events are occurring in other legislatures, you refuse to recognise that we conduct ourselves here with discipline, dignity, and goodwill. If you continue instead to rebuke and find fault with us, it can only mean that you are turning us toward the path I mentioned earlier.

We always work on a democratic basis. You may call it arrogance if you wish, but we did not enter this House intending to remain forever in opposition. If not today, we shall one day assume responsibility for governing. *(Cheers.)* We therefore recognise our responsibility, and I assure you that we shall not behave irresponsibly.

Next, I have prepared a note of the deficiencies raised by many members so that it may be useful to our Finance Minister. I have recorded not who said what, but what defects they identified. Many members repeatedly described conditions in their constituencies and asked whether their constituencies would receive attention, since none had been given so far. Many of those questions came from members of the ruling party. I am not now concerned about the others: when Congress members themselves suffer this predicament, nothing more need be said about everyone else's position.

One honourable lady member said, with deep emotion and tears, "We brought all sorts of ministers and explained our grievances to them. Finally, we even brought the Honourable Minister Kakkan and told him about them. We still have received no relief, and the tears of our suffering have not been wiped away." When you regard us poor members of the Opposition and think, "Is this even a party? What qualification do these people possess?" my heart ventures to ask why conditions remain so bad even after the Congress rulers have introduced all their fine reforms and excellent schemes.

### Images 5-6

Many members described how cultivators continued to suffer at the hands of landlords even after the sixty-forty law was enacted for their benefit. Passing laws alone is insufficient; the administrative machinery that implements them must be kept in proper repair. Members pointed out that in many places that machinery was not functioning correctly. They also drew the Government's attention to the need to distribute to cultivators the extensive lands held by monasteries and temples. Hearing all this, one is almost moved to say, "Alas, all these people should have been on our side, but the election separated them from us!" *(Laughter.)*

Hospitals have not been provided. Proper roads and transport have not been provided for village people. Electricity restrictions are severe. People face hardships arising from Ceylon. Since this morning, many members have asked for remedies to all these problems. Why should you become angry or distressed when we put these questions to you? We did not come here to sing you lullabies. We came to point out faults and deficiencies. That is the responsibility of democracy; we are not behaving without courtesy.

Many members have shown that unemployment has not been removed, irrigation facilities have not been provided, and drinking-water facilities have not been supplied.

What is the reason for all this? Congress members, I wish to tell you something through this House. Members of the Opposition naturally have grievances and must voice them so that remedies may be secured. But you, as members of the ruling party, have many committees through which you can inform the Government and have your grievances resolved. Why, despite that, do you raise them here? Have you grown weary of repeating them? Do your words fail to enter the ministers' ears? Or do you speak in our presence in the hope that the ministers will at least feel ashamed and remedy the defects? These are the questions that occur to me. If we did not have to discuss such grievances at length, we in the Opposition would have had more opportunity to discuss the budget's underlying policies.

All the charges made here amount to this: the administrative machinery is not functioning properly. If democracy is to grow, attention must be paid to the foundations of administrative policy. With your permission, I wish at this point to quote a passage from the Governor's address to this House:

> "The Finance Minister in his Budget Speech will deal with the policies of the new Ministry."

Because the Governor's address made that promise, one question is whether the Finance Minister's speech announced a corresponding policy. Another is whether you ask us, "Do you not know our policy?" I shall bring economics and political science together and present the figures.

### Images 7-8

You tell us to examine it. At school I studied the two subjects together as one. Even so, I regret to say that I cannot see anything here that amounts to an exposition of policy. As far as I have been able to discover, all the particulars presented in this budget were prepared under a committee. Only by looking at the three aspects considered by that committee can we properly judge the budget's relative merit.

For example, if Canada's ruling party presented a budget like this today, one might say that it did so in genuine fear, because the difference between government and opposition there is only ten or so. As for my party here, the ruling party will say that we number only fifteen.

Nor is that all. Even adding together everyone in the Opposition is difficult, and the ruling party has more than three times their combined strength. It therefore knows with certainty that, whatever defects are identified, whatever irregularities are exposed, and whatever policy is criticised, we can only speak today: what can our speaking accomplish, and what can possibly happen? I believe this budget was prepared in reliance on that confidence. I ask you to consider not merely our numbers, but our ideas.

Many members praised the Finance Minister so lavishly that their praise might give him indigestion. My own praise will mix a little spice with its sweetness, and may therefore cure that indigestion. I warmly congratulate the Finance Minister for conducting himself in a way that prevents us from discovering who he is. Perhaps he thought it unwise to reveal who he was before we found the reason for his concealment, or perhaps he wished to prevent us discovering it at all. I am speaking here in the language of economics.

Does he favour increasing the economy by imposing heavier taxation? Is he a traditional economist who would administer the country with the revenue available? Is he an economist who believes a deficit budget benefits the country? Or does he consider a surplus budget beneficial? His words do not tell us. When the deficit budget was discussed, some members praised it by saying, "Deficit Budget is not bad. It is a virtue also!"

The Honourable Mr Muthiah Chettiar hurriedly declared, "This is sound economics. Only now have they accepted the proposal I have advocated for many years." I wish to know whether the Minister intended to promote the country's welfare through deficit budgeting, or whether circumstances beyond his control forced him to present this budget. Everyone knows that there is a fundamental difference between the two.

### Images 9-10

Hitler, when ruling Germany, regarded a deficit budget as a powerful weapon in the Government's hands. Assuming unrestrained power, he printed vast quantities of German marks, distributed them with the support of the gun, and managed to stabilise and preserve his economy. Our Finance Minister has not explained the value he assigns to deficit budgeting. Wondering whether he perhaps believed a deficit budget intrinsically proper, I carefully examined the major speech he delivered on last year's budget.

The curious thing is that, just as he has been praised in this House today for presenting a deficit budget, he was praised in the same House then for imposing higher taxes. *(Laughter.)*

I believe the Finance Minister prepares each budget to suit his current position. Praise is natural in a democracy, but I sincerely regret that it has gone a little too far.

Some members welcomed the fact that he introduced no new taxes while presenting a deficit budget. As far as my party and I are concerned, we do not believe a country's hardship depends merely on whether taxation is high or low. The elementary economics I learned taught that taxes should be imposed on those able to bear them and reduced for those unable to do so. Perhaps the Finance Minister's advanced economics taught him something different, or perhaps he has acquired some novel administrative experience. *(Laughter.)* Economists have described the two approaches as "Progressive taxation" and "Regressive taxation"; in another classification they are explained as "Direct taxation" and "Indirect taxation."

Looking at the budget up to this moment, a considerable part of our revenue comes from the sales tax paid by poor people. How great an outcry has arisen throughout the country that so many fields have been neglected? At various stages we have shown that the sales tax truly torments the people. What all of us expected now was that the Finance Minister would at least exempt foodstuffs from sales tax.

For a long time, Tamil Nadu's merchants and trade associations have convened conferences, invited ministers, submitted petitions, and explained their grievances. They said, "We shall be satisfied if the sales tax imposed at several stages is instead collected at a single point." Because the Chief Minister comes from Virudhunagar, a town full of merchants, we thought he would act accordingly. Perhaps they are strict about sales tax because they fear that we might accuse them of favouring merchants. We shall make no such accusation. If they acted for some other reason, let that pass; but if they acted so because of us, please change course. I believe there should be a single-point sales tax and that foodstuffs should be exempted.

If the Finance Minister were to change "Foodgrains" to "Foodstuffs," the country would gain a measure of satisfaction.

### Images 11-12

His smile suggests that he will say our party's programme consists only of changing words and place names. *(Laughter.)* All the schemes we propose are schemes that cost nothing. If we proposed expensive schemes, where would they go? They go north, only to return disappointed when they find nothing there. *(Laughter.)*

If "food grains" were changed to "foodstuffs" and sales tax removed from them, revenue would fall by only a few lakhs of rupees. Yet whenever people ate, they would bless the Government wholeheartedly: "A good Government exists in this country; a good Ministry governs us; they have removed the tax on foodstuffs." They would value it as a gift of democracy, even as many people come and go in the Legislature. With the first mouthful they would bless the Chief Minister; with the second, the Finance Minister.

**Finance Minister C. Subramaniam:** With the third mouthful they will bless the man who proposed it. *(Laughter.)*

**Anna:** While eating, the public will unanimously offer thanks that their food is untaxed. The orthodox say it is good to bless others at mealtime. Though I differ from orthodoxy in other matters, I am willing to agree on this one.

Several Congress members who spoke here pointed to the absence of new taxes in the budget. My own view of why the Finance Minister imposed none is that the scar from the previous blow has not yet healed, and he may have decided not to strike the same place again. *(Laughter.)*

Finance ministers always suffer an itch of the mind. Seat a man in the Finance Minister's chair once, and taxes fill both his dreams and waking thoughts. I read a joke in a magazine: seeing the ordinary sari his wife was wearing, a Finance Minister reportedly asked, "Is there no tax on this?" Even there, his mind never left taxation. I refer to an imaginary Finance Minister, not this Finance Minister. *(Laughter.)*

Our Finance Minister has refrained from imposing taxes. We do not claim that he did so because he feared us. But because a few members of his party said as much, members of my movement replied, "Yes, it was indeed out of fear of us." I do not truly believe the Minister intends to leave the deficit untaxed. Members praised his statement for its fine Tamil; Tamil never lies. Consider its wording: "In this view, we have concluded that it is reasonable temporarily to leave the remaining deficit uncovered." Two or three dangerous words have been used there with great elegance.

By contrast, when Bombay State Finance Minister Jivraj Mehta presented a similar deficit budget, he explained: "The people of my State are already heavily taxed. I fear that a large share, as much as forty per cent, of the new tax imposed by the Central Government will be collected from my State. I have therefore introduced no new taxation proposals to cover the deficit in the budget."

## Part 2

### Images 1-2

Our Finance Minister, however, gave no reason. I eagerly searched to see whether he had supplied one, and his failure to do so aroused a fear in me. At last I found that, for reasons known to him, he had said, "We have concluded that it is reasonable temporarily to leave the remaining deficit uncovered." The word "temporarily" makes it seem that he is keeping us waiting until next year: "The blow fell only this year, and its scar has not yet disappeared. I shall give you another next year; be patient until then."

When we speak of taxation, only by examining the methods of taxation can we determine the course of a ruling party or government. That is why various parties, including progressive parties, have said that the programmes of those now governing this country are socialist, yet regretted that their conduct does not correspond to that claim.

The reason for that regret is clear. Our rulers keep saying "socialism, socialism," but what they practise today is not *samadharmam*, social equality, but *samaya dharmam*, expediency: they hold to whatever principle suits the moment. "Enough! This is no time to impose taxes, so let us impose none now." In that sense theirs is not socialism but merely the morality of the moment.

Considering the period in which this budget appears, the First Five-Year Plan has ended and we stand at the beginning of the Second. The first plan has improved our country to some extent, and the second will improve it further. Yet I cannot understand the argument that, from a sense of responsibility to complete the Second Five-Year Plan, the people must bear even new taxes.

I could understand and genuinely commend that argument only if figures showed that the First Five-Year Plan had truly benefited every poor and ordinary person, if the people accepted that proof, and if the strength they had gained through the first plan enabled them to bear the second.

Fortunately, as one who has practised medicine, I do not imagine that you, Mr Speaker, would give a strong tonic to a patient who was weak. Before undertaking the Second Five-Year Plan, we must ask whether we have adequately secured the three basic necessities: food, clothing, and shelter.

Mr Sriman Narayana, General Secretary of the Congress Party, discussed the taxes imposed for the Five-Year Plan in the Congress Party's *Economic Review*, which he described as almost an official journal. He wrote:

> "The People feel that while the poorer sections cannot escape taxation of an indirect nature in the form of sales tax and excise duties on various necessaries of life. The richer classes have become experts in managing evasion and avoidance of taxes."

### Images 3-4

Not content to rest on his own statement, he also cites the view of Professor Kalder, an economist:

> "According to Prof. Kalder's estimate evasion of direct taxes in India is of the order of Rs. 200 to 300 crores per year."

According to Professor Kalder's estimate, Mr Narayanan gives the figure for tax evasion in the Indian subcontinent as two hundred or three hundred crores of rupees a year. That is a very large amount. I also know that the Government of India says it may be only thirty or forty crores and, even on the most generous estimate, cannot exceed sixty crores. Nevertheless, Mr Narayanan has clearly shown that tax is being lost on this enormous scale.

Nor is that all. Referring particularly to the Five-Year Plan, he writes:

> "The common man has not been able to feel the glow of freedom during the last few years in accordance with his expectation."

When many people ask why the Government has failed to do this or that, members of the ruling party may complain, "Why do they do nothing but point out faults?" The people ask precisely because they have placed immense confidence in the Government. Members of the ruling party ought to take pride in that. Do not boast, "Under the British we had only hurricane lamps, whereas in our time large tube lights have been installed."

If you boast in that fashion, remember that the British ruler said much the same: "You had never even seen a train until I showed it to you, yet now you board that very train and tell me to leave." Concerned that Congress democracy should not turn from white imperialism into brown imperialism, Mr Narayanan says:

> "The fact remains that our plans and projects have not yet been able to touch visibly the poorest sections of the population."

I wish to add one more point. Discussing the Community Project under our Chief Minister's administration, Mr Narayanan says that it is not in a satisfactory condition. In implementing the scheme, we must examine how these farms are developed, from whom the selected lands will be purchased, and how the price of those lands will be fixed.

Instead of purchasing land, landlords could be summoned and legally required to establish seed farms on part of their extensive holdings. Since many landlords are friendly toward the ruling party, a kindly request to reserve part of their land for seed farms should induce them to provide it. We would then need to make no capital investment in those farms.

I believe the scheme can be implemented easily in this way. One member said that the seeds supplied do not reach poor cultivators. An honourable member drew on rural experience to explain the position clearly: seeds fail to arrive when farmers wait in distress, saying, "We need seed for sowing." They arrive only at harvest time and are therefore sold along with the harvested crop in the market.

### Images 5-6

A Government does not deserve praise merely for displaying plans on paper. We must ask how far each paper scheme has given every household one additional handful of rice, one additional spoonful of oil for the head, and one additional moment of peace in life compared with what it possessed before.

A contented smile is the praise and reward a Finance Minister should seek. The two problems of rising prices and declining foreign exchange can be solved only in relation to conditions throughout India. Recognising this well, the Minister has stated what can be done locally while explaining that we shall address them here too. Honourable members wanted us to praise him for that. One member asked emotionally, "Will you not say even one good thing?" If you can profit without causing us loss, why should we stand in your way?

That sort of thing is common in business. But I know our Finance Minister is not so fickle-minded as to be easily satisfied merely because one or two people praise him; that is why I refrain from praise. Some spoke appreciatively out of affection for him. That is good; let it stand.

The sound arrangements our Finance Minister has made in distributing what is available to him genuinely deserve a welcome. Yet, in welcoming them, we must divide the matter into three parts before delivering judgment: first, the proposed scheme; second, the money allocated to it; and third, the administrative method by which that money is spent. Only when all three are sound will our schemes produce genuine benefit.

For example, the Finance Minister said, "Thirty-eight seed farms were opened last year. We have decided to open 136 this year." He described this as a reasonable and necessary programme that the people would welcome. Yet Mr Narayana writes:

> "Even in the Community Projects and National Extension Services, it is now being admitted that the lowest sections of the rural population have not been benefited to any considerable extent."
>
> "Infact, all the available statistics tend to show that difference between the living standards of the richer and poorer sections instead of being narrowed down has increased further during the last 9 or 10 years."

I now ask this House, with the Honourable Speaker's permission: when the First Five-Year Plan has disappointed the people to this extent, what purpose is served by asking those same poor people to pay taxes? We must therefore judge this budget according both to whom it taxes and how it spends the revenue.

In paragraph five, the Finance Minister says, "Rising prices and the decline in foreign exchange are both..."

### Images 7-8

"...major problems affecting the country's economic condition today." He has stated this clearly. Anyone who denies it conceals the truth. There are indeed two major deficiencies today: rising prices and declining foreign exchange. I regret to say that our Finance Minister certainly possesses the ability to find sound remedies for both. Yet unless the two matters are administered directly under his personal supervision, no remedy will be effective. I am convinced of that.

We must accept on trust the Finance Minister's eager statement that eighty-eight seed farms were opened last year and that 136 will be opened this year. Judging from past performance, however, there is reason to doubt whether all 136 will actually open this year. Last year's budget promised fifty seed farms, but only thirty-eight were opened. If a promise of fifty produced thirty-eight, what shall we do if a promise of 136 stops at thirty-six? I pray to God: "Lord, do not give him such an idea; give him the determination to open every farm."

The budget also says that minor irrigation schemes will continue this year at a cost of fifty-five lakhs of rupees. Last year's budget figures show an allocation of one crore and twenty-three lakhs. Perhaps there are details I have missed because our Finance Minister has held the office for a long time while I am examining it for the first time. Yet he has a duty to explain the economic principle and political reason for allocating less this year than last.

Two major issues arose in the discussion of irrigation schemes. The first is the Chief Minister's repeated statement to the people: "There are no large rivers, so there is nowhere to build dams." He says, quite civilly, that we possess no great rivers on which dams can be constructed. But some in his party say, "There are no rivers to dam; it is Annadurai's mouth that must be dammed." Build one as far as you can; I do not forbid you.

When we discuss minor irrigation today, however, many opportunities remain to control the mountain streams still running to waste and create small irrigation works. We are told that these are set aside because the direct revenue obtained from the expenditure would be very small. Officials consider only the immediate profit. If they converted into rupees the happiness and satisfaction such projects would create among the people, I believe they would adopt a policy of completing every one of them.

### Images 9-10

If I say nothing about my constituency, its people will not think that Annadurai spoke only about the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam and ignored them. Members of the Opposition here, however, may say so. I therefore wish to bring a dry river in my constituency to the moist heart of the Chief Minister.

When I was a schoolboy, a teacher once rang the bell and announced, "Children may go home; water has reached the Palar." In our region, water appearing in the Palar is an event worthy of a school holiday and celebration. Yet it is a fine, large river. Once a year, I tell the Speaker, three or four days of rain bring a flood. The water is caught in small reservoirs at various places, fills the great tanks and ponds, and supports irrigation in Chingleput. I must point out two deficiencies.

Because the floodwater is stored in tanks, the silt it carries settles there and reduces their depth. In time the water breaks through the channels, and even when a flood comes the tank cannot hold it but disgorges it outside. Just as we sometimes study matters without understanding their true meaning and wrongly pour them back out, the tank pours away the surging water.

Whenever we ask what stage has been reached in the many rounds of negotiation between the Madras and Mysore Governments, we are told that the matter is closed. Our administration should not regard any such problem as closed. The Chief Minister of our State must reopen discussions on the Palar with the Chief Minister of Mysore. Experts spoke about it earlier. Our Chief Minister Kamaraj is not an expert, and the people place such confidence in him today precisely because he is not one. Experts trust statistics; he learns the country's condition by looking at the people's hungry stomachs, parched heads, and careworn faces. Negotiations with Mysore must therefore be resumed immediately so that water reaches the Palar.

If that is impossible, Russia offers another method. Even where no surface water remains in a river, subsoil water is raised from underground at different points by electrical power and used for irrigation. Our Engineering Department may be able to explain that system. I urge that water be brought to the Palar in this way.

Bring water to the Palar, and people will garland you even if you travel to every corner of Chingleput district denouncing our movement. They will say, "Let him criticise them as much as he likes; the ruler brought us water." There are two advantages: you gain the people's support, and you gain a means of restraining us. All that matters today is bringing water to the Palar. Necessary action must be taken.

**Mr Speaker:** I suppose the Hon. Member will finish his speech soon.

**Sri A. A. Rasheed:** How much more time, the Hon. Member will take?

**Mr Speaker:** I hope the Hon. Member will finish reasonably quickly.

### Images 11-12 And Recovered Image 13

**Anna:** When presenting last year's budget to this House, the Finance Minister made an appeal. I wish to read it here:

> "But there is one aspect that truly causes us profound concern. In our Indian national plan, a large part of the total expenditure will be used to develop basic industries such as iron and steel and, in step with that growth, to improve transport facilities. About two thousand crores of rupees is planned for these items in the public sector. It does not appear that any significant portion of this enormous sum will be spent in South India. It is true that we have no coal, but that does not alter the fact that the national plan contains no significant effort toward the industrial development of the southern region.
>
> "About fifty crores of rupees has been allocated to the Neyveli lignite project. Even including those fifty crores, the proportion spent on the southern states will not rise greatly. Moreover, the Neyveli project is still at an experimental stage. The southern region contains many suitable sites for establishing major engineering industries. When sites for those industries are selected, it is essential that this region receive at least some consideration. Taking into account both the population dependent on the land and the cultivated acreage, nowhere else in the country has population pressure equal to that of this region.
>
> "There is a proposal to establish a heavy electrical engineering factory. We can provide all the facilities it requires. Our electricity grid has enough power to produce aluminium from the bauxite deposits at Salem. There is also room here to establish a factory making heavy machinery from the steel that is to be produced.
>
> "We have pressed our position on these questions. I am confident that if this House joins me in supporting it more forcefully, we can ensure that the Planning Commission and the Government of India give proper attention to these matters, which are indispensable to our economic development."

At the end he also makes an appeal: "My hands must be strengthened if all this is to be accomplished." If he genuinely seeks the cooperation of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam in developing this country's industries, the movement is ready to provide it today. The Opposition will always cooperate with industrial development that allows workers to live without injury, increases the country's wealth, and at the same time transforms the capitalist class.

But it must be decided when and at what stage the rulers ask for that cooperation. They repeatedly say that the Opposition must cooperate, but do not make clear when or at what stage they seek it.

I believe we are ready if cooperation is requested while plans are being framed. I shall put it in terms of the *Ramayana*, thinking that those present will then be satisfied. Did not even the squirrel help Rama? In the same way, Annadurai too may be of some use. *(Laughter.)* I repeat that our cooperation is assured if you ask for it when plans are first devised. And I temporarily praise the Finance Ministry for temporarily imposing no tax in this budget.

---NOTES---
All embedded English passages damaged by Tamil-only OCR on Part 1 Images 6 and 8-10 and Part 2 Images 2-4, 6, and 10 were transcribed directly from the scans. Their printed capitalization, grammar, and spellings, including `Kalder`, `The People`, and `Infact`, are retained verbatim.

The source uses `சமதர்மம்` and the wordplay `சமய தர்மம்`; these are rendered as “social equality/socialism” and “expediency/the morality of the moment” according to context.

The “sixty-forty law” refers to the cultivator-landlord crop-sharing arrangement described in the source; its colloquial designation is retained rather than expanded into an uncertain formal statute name.
